//? Title: Batch Normalisation Ablation: Paired Tensor Training
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A change to a CNN layer can appear useful simply because the two runs started with different
weights or saw different minibatches. We compare a plain MNIST CNN with its batch-normalised
counterpart using matched kernel values, seeds and row orders. Every Adam update and
validation selection remains visible, including the non-trained moving-statistics state.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.1_fundamentals/batch_norm_ablation.cpp`.

> The method

Training normalises each channel over batch and spatial axes, then applies learned gamma and
beta before ReLU. Inference uses moving statistics instead of the current batch. With momentum
m=.9, the state update is mean←m mean+(1-m) batch_mean and similarly for variance; the library
stores the unbiased batch variance in that moving variance, while normalisation uses population
variance. Epsilon .001 stabilises the denominator.

$$
\widehat x=\frac{x-\mu_B}{\sqrt{v_B+\epsilon}},\qquad
\Delta_s=A_{s,BN}-A_{s,plain}.
$$
\alttext(Batch normalisation centres and scales each channel before gamma and beta; paired accuracy differences compare runs sharing the same seed and initial kernels.)

The two models retain identical convolution biases, kernel L2 and dense layers. BN adds
2(16+32)=96 trainable scalars, plus 96 moving-state scalars that Adam does not update. Three
paired seeds provide descriptive differences rather than a general advantage claim. Each
model fits 128 original MNIST rows for two epochs, selects on the following 64 rows, then
uses 64 original test rows once. These short subsets differ from the original full ablation.
Normalisation costs O(BHWC) per layer, and its training output depends on batch companions.
Inference must neither change state nor depend on unrelated companion images.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/batch_norm_ablation_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/batch_norm_ablation_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/batch_norm_ablation_concept.tikz > build/batch_norm_ablation_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/batch_norm_ablation_concept.svg build/batch_norm_ablation_concept.dvi > build/batch_norm_ablation_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
